        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Walking Pads</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these walking pads, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. The picks were made from published specifications, manufacturer and retailer
            listings, warranty terms and independent coverage, checked on 13 October 2026. For each
            pad we confirmed that it is currently sold, on the brand's own store, on Amazon, at a
            specialist retailer, or several of these.
          </p>
          <p>
            The starting filter was a stated capacity of roughly 300 lb or more. That alone removes
            most of the category. What removed most of the rest was the second test: <strong>does
            the capacity hold up across sources?</strong> We compared the brand's product page with
            the Amazon or retailer listing and, where we could reach it, with a spec tracker that
            reads owner's manuals. A capacity printed on a card below is one where at least two of
            those agreed. Several pads sold as 350 or 400 lb did not make it, because the manual or
            a second official listing gave a lower figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond capacity, we looked for <strong>a motor rating that means something</strong> —
            ideally a published continuous figure rather than a peak horsepower number alone —
            <strong>a belt wide enough for a broader stance</strong>, and <strong>a seller with
            written warranty terms</strong>. We also searched the US Consumer Product Safety
            Commission's notices for every brand on the shortlist. Sperax, which sells walking pads
            marketed at high capacities, is excluded for that reason: in April 2026 the CPSC warned
            consumers to stop using Sperax walking pads and treadmills, citing reports of
            uncontrolled speed changes, falls and overheating.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some pads you may have seen recommended elsewhere are missing on purpose. LifeSpan's
            TR1200 desk treadmill is quoted at 330 lb, 350 lb and 400 lb in different places,
            depending on which version and which listing you read, and the current model was sold
            out on LifeSpan's own store. WalkingPad's A1 Pro is sold in both 230 lb and 300 lb
            versions under the same name, and the 300 lb version was not reliably in stock. Pads
            from little-known brands advertising 440 or 450 lb ratings had no manufacturer page or
            independent coverage we could check them against.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-margin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why Capacity Margin Matters on a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            A weight capacity is the most heavily marketed number on a high-capacity pad and the
            least well defined. Brands do not publish how they arrive at it, whether it assumes
            walking or running, or how long the machine is expected to last at that load. Treat it
            as a ceiling the manufacturer will stand behind under warranty, not a comfortable
            operating point.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rule of thumb in our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity guide</a>
            is to buy a machine rated at least 50 lb above your weight if you only walk, and more if
            you will jog or if several people will use it. On a walking pad that margin matters more
            than on a full treadmill, for three reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Everything is smaller.</strong> A pad's rollers, bearings, deck and motor are
            sized to keep the machine light enough to slide under a sofa. A full treadmill rated
            for 350 lb has a heavier frame, larger rollers and a bigger motor than a pad with the
            same printed figure, and so it has more in reserve when it is used near that figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pads are used for long sessions.</strong> A desk walker may be on the belt for
            two, four or six hours a day. That is more running time than most home treadmills see,
            at a steady load, with the motor working hardest at the slow speeds desk walking uses.
            Heat is what wears motors and belts, and a long session close to the rated maximum
            produces a lot of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The rating may depend on speed.</strong> UREVO is unusually candid about this:
            its FoldiMix 5L page quotes 400 lb "for normal operation at regular speed" and 300 lb
            for its maximum-speed test. Most brands give a single number and leave you to guess.
            If a pad offers a running mode, assume the safe load at running speed is lower than the
            headline capacity, and check the manual for a separate figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then there is the question of whether the number is real at all. In this category the
            same product is often listed at different capacities in different places. On one pad on
            this page, the brand says 400 lb while European listings say 136 kg, about 300 lb. On a
            pad we excluded, the US listing, the Amazon listing and the European manual gave three
            different figures between 300 and 400 lb. In our list of
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline</a>,
            one maker's own pages quoted both 330 and 400 lb for the same pad. When figures
            conflict, plan on the lowest one, and get the seller to confirm the rating for your
            exact model number in writing before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put together, a practical approach looks like this. If you weigh up to about 250 lb,
            a confirmed 300 lb pad leaves a sensible margin for walking. Between about 250 and 300 lb,
            look at the 350 and 400 lb pads on this page. Above about 300 to 320 lb, even a 400 lb
            pad leaves less margin than we would like for daily, hours-long use, and a full
            treadmill built for heavier users is the better choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-frame-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Deck, Frame and Belt Width</h2>
<p>
            Capacity is a single number, and the things that actually carry your weight are not.
            Three are worth checking on any high-capacity pad.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deck.</strong> Under the belt is a board that takes the force of every step.
            Most pads do not say what it is made of or how thick it is. LifeSpan does — the TX6 uses
            a 0.7-inch phenolic deck — and independent coverage of the W50 describes a reinforced deck about 15 mm thick.
            A thicker, denser deck flexes less under load, which is better for the belt, the motor
            and your joints alike. A brand that publishes deck details is at least telling you it
            has thought about them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The frame and the machine's own weight.</strong> Heavier is generally better here.
            The TX6 weighs 98.5 lb by LifeSpan's figure, with aluminium side rails; the MX8 weighs
            about 74 lb; the W50 about 69 lb. Budget pads rated at 265 lb often weigh 40 to 50 lb.
            A pad's weight is a rough proxy for how much metal is in the frame, and a frame that is
            stiff under you is quieter, tracks the belt better and shrugs off the sideways loads
            of a heavier walker's stride. The trade-off is that you have to move it, and nothing
            above 70 lb is pleasant to lift alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt width.</strong> This is the dimension heavier walkers notice most and
            listings mention least. Many pads have a belt about 15 to 16 inches wide. That is
            enough for a narrow, straight walking line, but a broader body usually means a slightly
            wider stance, and on a 15-inch belt the feet of a wide-stance walker end up close to
            the edges, where a scuff on the frame can break your stride. The W50 runs a 16.5-inch
            belt; the TX6 runs a 20-inch belt, the same width as many full treadmills. If you have
            ever felt your feet drift toward the side rails, prioritise width over length.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt length</strong> matters too, especially for taller walkers, and the
            general advice in our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            applies here unchanged: 40 inches or more suits most adults at desk pace, and longer is
            more forgiving if you drift backwards while concentrating on a screen. The TX6's
            48-inch belt is the longest on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-rating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Motor: Read the Continuous Rating, Not the Peak</h2>
<p>
            Almost every pad advertises its motor in horsepower, and almost every one of those
            figures is a peak rating — what the motor can produce briefly, not what it can sustain.
            Pads marketed at 2.5, 3.0 or 3.5 HP routinely turn out, on the motor label or in the
            manual, to have continuous ratings well under 1 HP. For a heavier walker that gap
            matters, because a heavier load at a slow speed is exactly the condition that asks a
            motor for sustained torque.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two pads on this page make the point neatly, because they come from the same brand.
            MERACH publishes both figures for its W50 TrekPad — 3.5 HP peak and 1.25 HP continuous
            — and for the W50 Lite — 3.0 HP peak and 0.75 HP continuous. The Lite's capacity is
            50 lb lower, but its continuous rating is 40% lower. If you are near the top of the
            Lite's range and plan long sessions, the TrekPad's larger continuous rating is the more
            important difference, even though the peak figures on the box look similar.
          </p>
          <p>
            A few things to look for:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A continuous (CHP) figure stated by the brand.</strong> Treat it with some
            caution, but a brand that publishes one is easier to hold to account than one that
            publishes only peak horsepower.</li>
            <li><strong>A brushless motor,</strong> where independently confirmed. Brushless motors
            run cooler and have no brushes to wear, which suits long daily sessions. Several budget
            brands claim brushless motors that teardowns have found to be brushed, so a claim on its
            own is not proof.</li>
            <li><strong>A stated daily-use rating.</strong> LifeSpan's TX6 is described for up to
            about seven hours of use a day. Most pads give no figure at all; a manual that sets a
            maximum session length is telling you something worth knowing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our explainer on <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC and DC treadmill motors</a>
            covers how motor ratings work in more detail. The short version for a pad: ignore the
            peak horsepower, look for a continuous figure, and expect a pad with no published
            continuous rating to have a modest one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handrail-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Should You Get a Handrail Model?</h2>
<p>
            A handrail on a walking pad is a steadying aid, not a support you can lean on. That
            distinction matters more for a heavier walker than for anyone else, for two reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            First, a pad's rail or handles are fixed to a light deck with a few bolts. They are fine
            for resting a hand on, for steadying yourself while the belt changes speed, and for
            stepping on and off. They are not designed to carry a large share of your body weight,
            and leaning on them hard and often loosens fixings and stresses the joint where the
            rail meets the deck. Our guide to
            <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether to hold the handrails</a>
            explains when holding on is sensible and when it gets in the way.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, on many 2-in-1 pads, raising the rail is what unlocks higher speeds. If you are
            buying a rail mainly to feel more secure while walking, that is a good reason. If you
            are buying it to jog, remember that the capacity at running speed may be lower than the
            headline figure — UREVO's own numbers for the FoldiMix 5L show exactly that.
          </p>
          <p>
            The two handrail-style pads on this page take different approaches. The WalkingPad MX8
            has removable side handles, one on each side, so you can hold on with either hand
            without reaching forward. The UREVO FoldiMix 5L has a single front rail with a desk tray
            that folds down flat. Side handles suit someone who wants a hand free to steady
            themselves at walking pace; a front rail suits someone who wants a desk surface and the
            option to jog. Our list of the
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with a handrail</a>
            covers the wider category.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you need real support — a rail you can hold throughout, on both sides, that will
            take some of your weight if you stumble — no pad provides it. That is a job for a full
            treadmill with proper side rails, covered in the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="full-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. When a Full Treadmill Is the Better Choice</h2>
<p>
            A high-capacity pad is a good answer to a specific question: how to walk at a desk, or in
            a small room, at a weight most pads are not built for. It is the wrong answer to several
            others, and for heavier users the line is worth drawing early.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 300 to 320 lb,</strong> the margin even a 400 lb
            pad leaves is thinner than we would like for daily use. Full treadmills with confirmed
            ratings of 350 to 400 lb have larger motors, longer and wider decks, heavier frames and
            longer warranties. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a>
            covers those machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want to jog or run,</strong> a pad is a poor fit at any weight, and the
            impact of running multiplies the load on a light deck. Our page on
            <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you can run on a walking pad</a>
            explains the limits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need something solid to hold,</strong> buy a treadmill with full-length
            side rails and a low step-up height. The
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for seniors guide</a>
            covers machines built around stability, and most of its advice applies to anyone who
            wants a rail they can rely on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If several people will share the machine,</strong> size it for the heaviest
            regular user and apply the margin to them. A pad bought for one person's desk often
            becomes the household's treadmill, and the capacity has to cover whoever uses it most.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A note on health.</strong> We review equipment; this page is not medical advice.
            Walking is one of the most accessible ways to be more active at any weight, but if you
            are starting exercise after a long break, or you have a joint, heart, balance or other
            condition, check with your doctor about how much and how fast to begin. Start with
            short sessions at a slow speed and build up, and stop if you feel pain, dizziness or
            chest discomfort.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you are still weighing up a pad against a treadmill in general, our comparison of
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads and treadmills</a>
            is a better starting point than any list of products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the highest weight capacity for a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The highest ratings we could confirm across more than one source were 400 lb, on the MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad and the LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp. Some listings advertise 440 or 450 lb, but we could not find a manufacturer page or independent coverage to check those against, so we have not recommended them. Most walking pads are rated between 220 and 265 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 300 lb person use a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on a pad with a confirmed rating well above 300 lb and a decent continuous motor rating, such as a 400 lb model, and for walking rather than running. Leave a margin of at least 50 lb, plan on the lowest capacity figure if listings disagree, and start with shorter sessions. Above about 320 lb, a full treadmill rated for heavier users is the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight capacity margin should I leave?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 50 lb above your weight for walking, and more if you will jog, use it for long daily sessions or share it with others. A pad's rollers, deck and motor are small, so running it at its limit wears it faster than the same load on a full treadmill. If two listings for the same pad disagree on capacity, use the lower figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do walking pad weight capacities differ between listings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget pads are often sold under one name across a brand site, Amazon and other retailers, with listings written separately and specifications revised without a new model name. Some brands also rate capacity differently at walking and running speeds, and a few sell two capacity versions under the same name. Confirm the rating for your exact model number with the seller in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad with a handrail better for heavy people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can help with balance, especially when stepping on and off or when the speed changes, but a pad's rail or handles are a steadying aid, not a support to lean on. They are bolted to a light deck and not built to carry much of your weight. If you need a rail you can rely on, a full treadmill with side rails is safer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does motor horsepower matter for heavier users?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but the continuous rating matters, not the peak figure on the box. A heavier walker at slow speed asks the motor for sustained torque, which is what the continuous rating describes. Many pads advertised at 2.5 to 3.5 HP have continuous ratings under 1 HP. Look for a brand that publishes a continuous figure, such as MERACH's 1.25 HP on the W50 TrekPad.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Walking Pad as a Heavier User</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two of these come from trusting a single number on a listing, and two from asking a pad to do a treadmill’s job.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a pad rated exactly at your weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A pad rated at 300 lb is at its limit with a 300 lb walker on it, every session. On a machine with small rollers and a light deck that shows up as belt wear, motor heat and a shorter life. Leave at least 50 lb of margin for walking and more for long daily use, and if that pushes you past 400 lb, look at a full treadmill.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the highest capacity figure you can find</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">High-capacity pads are often listed at different ratings in different places, and the highest one is usually on the marketplace listing. Check the brand’s own page and, where you can, the manual. If they disagree, plan on the lowest figure, and ask the seller to confirm the rating for your exact model number in writing before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the motor by peak horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 3.5 HP figure on the box is almost always a peak rating. What carries a heavier walker through a long session is the continuous rating, which on many pads is under 1 HP. Prefer a brand that publishes a continuous figure, and treat a pad with only a peak number as having a modest motor.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaning on a pad’s handrail for support</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A fold-up rail or side handles on a pad are bolted to a light deck and designed for a steadying hand. Leaning on them hard loosens fixings and puts strain where the rail meets the frame. If you need to hold on throughout or take weight off your legs, a treadmill with full-length side rails is the right machine.</p>
